; smoke.eval — the harness proving it can drive the game unattended.
;
; Run:  Dungeon.exe -eval <path to this file>
; Exit: 0 if every line matched a command and the queue emptied.
;
; It starts at the TITLE SCREEN, so the first job is getting into a world. The
; runner holds each line back while a staged load is in flight, so there is no
; wait directive here and nothing to tune — `reset` is simply followed by the
; next line once the world actually exists.
;
; `reset` rather than `newgame`: as the FIRST script in a process it loads (there
; is nothing to recycle), and as a later one in a batch it recycles in ~340 ms
; instead of reloading in ~12 s. Every suite opens this way now.

logecho on
state

; Into a world. The lines after this one wait for the load by themselves.
reset
state

; THE TWO RULES (docs/eval-harness.md "Driving it"). timescale 0 means normal
; frames advance the world by nothing, so ONLY `step` moves time and the
; measurement cannot be contaminated by however long the frames took. lockstep
; means the monsters actually think during that step instead of walking a
; cached path toward where the party used to be.
timescale 0
lockstep on
seed 7

; Stand next to the skeleton in the north corridor and take twenty seconds of it.
tp 12 8
echo --- before ---
party
monsters
step 20
echo --- after ---
party
monsters
state
